      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended on a count that doubles every hour, two to the x. Money can double too, just more slowly. Put a thousand dollars into a savings account that doubles every ten years. Count in thousands of dollars and in decades, and the balance is two to the x. Here it is beside e to the x. Both start at a height of one. E to the x leaves that point with a slope of exactly one, because it's its own derivative. But two to the x is flatter there. So the derivative of two to the x can't just be two to the x. Then how fast is the balance really growing? Here's the strange part. The answer contains a logarithm. Why would a log turn up when you differentiate an exponential?"
    },
